[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e75919e2-598b-5ed9-b995-054b0382a274",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + 2 x + 1$ at $\\left(-1,1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} + 2 x + 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 2\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=4 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=-2\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-2\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(1\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "e99e6999-3bcd-5712-9fcf-770009234848",
          ":answer-field/key": "slope",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "e073641c-52a2-56cb-b5e3-e1a8667ec161",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{1}{2}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "e073641c-52a2-56cb-b5e3-e1a8667ec161"
          ]
        },
