    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c80c6849-c2ca-5979-b8db-38c19e3845b0",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - 2 x + 2$ at $\\left(2,6\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} - 2 x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x - 2\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=4 \\left(2\\right) - 2=6\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(6\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(6\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{6}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{6}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(6\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{19}{3} - \\frac{x}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{1}{6}$ and $b=\\frac{19}{3}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "49a01165-2103-5d49-b38b-1c4c39ac14cd",
          ":answer-field/key": "slope",
