    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e1d1e8e1-6c58-5feb-8546-1c89ed2de14b",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 - x^{2}$ at $\\left(2,-2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 - x^{2}$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 2 x\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=- 2 \\left(2\\right)=-4\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-4\\right)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-2\\right)&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{4}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $b=- \\frac{5}{2}$.",
