    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2a9bdbd1-919f-5e31-bb77-2aeaec04df9f",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^{2} - x + 2$ at $\\left(0,2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=x^{2} - x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 x - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(0\\right)=2 \\left(0\\right) - 1=-1\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-1\\right)}=1.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(2\\right)&=1\\left(x-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=x+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\ny&=x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=1$ and $b=2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
