    "question": {
      ":question/id": "91cfc5c5-3294-5b71-b541-308878f23905",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{2} + 2 x + 2$ at $\\left(-1,-1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- x^{2} + 2 x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 - 2 x\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=2 - 2 \\left(-1\\right)=4\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(4\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-1\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{4}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{4}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{4} - \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{1}{4}$ and $b=- \\frac{5}{4}$.",
