  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7a8a6230-8630-5356-ac9d-7314af4d4c0e",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^{2} + x + 2$ at $\\left(-1,2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=x^{2} + x + 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 x + 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=2 \\left(-1\\right) + 1=-1\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-1\\right)}=1.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(2\\right)&=1\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=x+\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\ny&=x + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=1$ and $b=3$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "99c8a247-5dfb-532d-b8df-e3aa7c31b060",
