    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0871088e-7d56-5e47-8fe6-aaaa735fe8e4",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + x - 1$ at $\\left(2,9\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} + x - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=4 \\left(2\\right) + 1=9\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(9\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(9\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{9}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{9}+\\left(\\frac{2}{9}\\right)+\\left(9\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{83}{9} - \\frac{x}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{1}{9}$ and $b=\\frac{83}{9}$.",
