    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8d214aa5-8bc1-5b7f-8f97-ee90c39caed7",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - 2$ at $\\left(-1,0\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} - 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=4 \\left(-1\\right)=-4\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-4\\right)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(0\\right)&=\\frac{1}{4}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{4}+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $b=\\frac{1}{4}$.",
