  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0ca3776b-80ad-530c-bb72-66db6ee0031e",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - 2$ at $\\left(2,6\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} - 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(2\\right)=4 \\left(2\\right)=8\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(8\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(6\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{8}\\left(x-\\left(2\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{8}+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+\\left(6\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{25}{4} - \\frac{x}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{1}{8}$ and $b=\\frac{25}{4}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c649f965-1a66-5ca4-927f-81794e887e1c",
