    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b3123edb-b938-5f99-a1bf-a9925d2b6684",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} + x$ at $\\left(-1,-3\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- 2 x^{2} + x$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=1 - 4 x\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=1 - 4 \\left(-1\\right)=5\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(5\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-3\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{5}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{5}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{5}\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{5} - \\frac{16}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{1}{5}$ and $b=- \\frac{16}{5}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
