    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2c5190ec-788d-57dd-ba0a-ac868b7de9a9",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} + x - 1$ at $\\left(-1,0\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} + x - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x + 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=4 \\left(-1\\right) + 1=-3\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-3\\right)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(0\\right)&=\\frac{1}{3}\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{3}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $b=\\frac{1}{3}$.",
