      ":question/id": "017f5f70-2ecc-52e8-9c6d-9989f1ba6d47",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x - 2$ at $\\left(0,-2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- 2 x^{2} + 2 x - 2$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 - 4 x\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(0\\right)=2 - 4 \\left(0\\right)=2\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(2\\right)}=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-2\\right)&=- \\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{2}+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{x}{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $b=-2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5f3ff0ac-2100-5d62-93b1-21aba107d54c",
          ":answer-field/key": "slope",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
