  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092240,
      "id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "308dd57e-9a71-5ac2-ac3b-2c1326e5839f",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- 2 x^{2} - x$ at $\\left(0,0\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- 2 x^{2} - x$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 4 x - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(0\\right)=- 4 \\left(0\\right) - 1=-1\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-1\\right)}=1.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(0\\right)&=1\\left(x-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=x+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\ny&=x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=1$ and $b=0$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "731cd8f2-186d-5554-8c55-6e8fb7210de2",
