      "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bfc22883-0346-5891-81f5-fd16735b1d14",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 x^{2} - 2 x - 1$ at $\\left(0,-1\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 x^{2} - 2 x - 1$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=4 x - 2\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(0\\right)=4 \\left(0\\right) - 2=-2\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-2\\right)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(-1\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2}+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(-1\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{x}{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $b=-1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c0734c9c-824f-538c-b765-46effa9af8a0",
          ":answer-field/key": "slope",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
