    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5b31e973-1348-5e18-80de-622159d8dfee",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=- x^{2} - x$ at $\\left(-1,0\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=- x^{2} - x$. Differentiate:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=- 2 x - 1\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(-1\\right)=- 2 \\left(-1\\right) - 1=1\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(1\\right)}=-1.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(0\\right)&=-1\\left(x-\\left(-1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- x+\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\ny&=- x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=-1$ and $b=-1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
